None of the checkpoint-stop consumers needed a code change. I added 6 tests that pin the current behaviour on yoagent 0.27.5 and committed them as 01c8b118, titled "Fixes #1159". Task 1 and the lock bump are both in the log, so this completes #1159's done-when. cargo test, cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings and cargo fmt -- --check all pass.

How the tests work: they live in the new src/checkpoint_stop_tests.rs, registered in src/main.rs. Each one runs yoyo's real prompt path on a run that ends the way 0.27.5 now ends it. A stub provider replies with some text and one tool call, and the stop fires before the next turn, which is where the checkpoint limit trips in practice. The marker text is built from yoagent's exported AGENT_STOPPED_PREFIX rather than typed in.

| What reads the stop | Result on 0.27.5 |
|---|---|
| Message history (control) | The last message is the stop marker, right after the tool result. If yoagent stops adding it, this test fails first. |
| -p and --output-format json | Both carry the model's reply, not the marker. The marker never enters the reply text. |
| Exit code | Still 2. The stopped run doesn't count as failed or incomplete; the same run without the checkpoint flag exits 0. |
| --save-session then --continue | The marker is kept, and the next prompt gets an answer. The history then has two user messages in a row. |
| REPL stop line | The marker adds no second "stopped" line. Only the existing "⚡ Context at N% — checkpoint-restart triggered" message appears. |
| [Agent stopped: checks | yoyo has only one, and it reads sub-agent results. The main agent's reply never contains the marker, so it isn't misread. |

Limits:
• The tests use a stop function of the same shape as the real checkpoint one, not the real one. The real one is written inline inside build_agent and can't be called on its own.
• The tests don't set the process-wide CHECKPOINT_TRIGGERED flag, because an existing test checks its default. They pass its value straight to the exit-code function instead.
• The resume row was checked against yoagent's mock provider, which accepts two user turns in a row. My understanding is that Anthropic's API merges back-to-back user turns, but I didn't check that against their docs or run a real provider request.

Filed separately as #1161 (from reading the code, not from a run): in the REPL, auto-continue is on by default and doesn't check for a checkpoint stop. It can send "Continue…" and compact the context, so checkpoint mode gets quietly replaced by compaction. If compaction can't shrink the context, the stop fires again and prints a second stop line. This predates 0.27.5 and isn't part of #1159's done-when.
